  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The place</h2>
      <p>La Halle de Grimaud, 56 chemin de la Rine, is an organic, local grocery in the countryside at Grimaud. It brings together a fruit and vegetable section, a butcher-deli, dairy and cheese, eggs from its own farm and a wine cellar, and it also caters. The Grimaud tourist office adds a fishmonger: a stall offers fish, shellfish and crustaceans every day, and a seafood platter can be ordered or eaten on site, especially on Sundays, when a musical atmosphere sets in.</p>

      <h2>Eating on site</h2>
      <p>The tourist office listing mentions a tasting terrace available to customers, and an oyster bar every weekend in a guinguette atmosphere. This is a grocery with a tasting area, not a restaurant with table service: you come to taste products bought on site. Accepted payments are cash, bank card and meal vouchers. The site, counter and toilets are described as accessible to people with reduced mobility, with a hard surface and parking.</p>

      <h2>With a dog</h2>
      <p>The "Animals accepted" mention appears among the services in the Grimaud tourist office listing (relayed by Visit Var, updated on 09/02/2026, and by Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur Tourisme). It concerns the establishment as a whole: it does not say whether animals are admitted in the aisles, on the terrace or at the oyster bar. The weekend oyster bar is lively, which may matter for a noise-sensitive dog; on weekdays, the atmosphere is calmer.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: call 04 94 56 06 80 before coming to find out where your dog can settle, especially at a weekend.</div>

      <h2>Opening hours</h2>
      <p>Visit Var and the tourist office announce opening every day from 8am to 7:30pm, all year, specifying that days and hours should be checked with the establishment. A market directory instead indicates opening Tuesday to Sunday: the two versions differ. Website: lahalledegrimaud.com.</p>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The medieval village of Grimaud and its lanes, as well as the lakeside town of Port Grimaud, are a few minutes away by car to extend the outing.</p>
